"                \"group\": [\n"
"                    \"...\"\n"
"                ]\n"
"            }\n"
"        },\n"
"        \"...\": \"...\"\n"
"    }\n"
"}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ethdebug.rst:88
msgid "Type Documents"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ethdebug.rst:90
msgid ""
"The ``types`` table maps type identifiers to type documents (schema "
"``ethdebug/format/type``). The compiler currently uses the identifiers "
"its :ref:`storage layout <storage-layout-top-level>` output uses as well, "
"such as ``t_uint256``, ``t_array$_t_uint8_$dyn_storage`` or "
"``t_struct$_Point_$6_storage``. The only guarantee about the identifier is "
"that it is a string that is unique within the table. Its exact form is an "
"implementation detail, but it is generally based on the name used in type "
"definition. When that name is not unique within the compilation, it may be "
"disambiguated with additional information, such as the AST ID of the "
"definition."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ethdebug.rst:96
msgid "The table contains documents for the types of:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ethdebug.rst:98
msgid ""
"all state variables, including inherited variables, constants and immutables,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ethdebug.rst:99
msgid "all file-level constants in the analyzed sources,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ethdebug.rst:100
msgid ""
"parameters, return variables and local variables of functions and modifiers "
"in each contract's creation and deployed call graphs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ethdebug.rst:102
msgid ""
"The last group includes constructors, public and external functions, "
"fallback and receive functions, their modifiers, and the internal functions "
"they call, including free and library functions. An internal function also "
"contributes its variable types if it is assigned to a function variable "
"(``callback = f``) or merely evaluated (``f;``), even without a call. "
"Internal functions called or evaluated in state-variable initializers are "
"included as well. Collection happens before optimization, so it also "
"includes declarations in code that is later removed. Parameters and return "
"variables of interface functions and other unimplemented functions are "
"included even though those declarations have no bytecode. Locals include "
"variables in nested blocks and loops and the success and failure parameters "
"of ``try``/``catch`` clauses."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ethdebug.rst:109
msgid ""
"The tables are merged across all analyzed contracts and sources, not just "
"the contracts selected for bytecode output. A selective import therefore "
"does not exclude types contributed by other contracts or file-level "
"constants in the imported source. Taking an external function's ``selector`` "
"or ``address`` does not add its variable types to the caller's table, but "
"the function's own contract or interface still contributes them to the "
"merged table."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ethdebug.rst:113
msgid ""
"Event and error parameters do not independently contribute types. Neither do "
"unused type definitions or types mentioned only in expressions, such as "
"conversions, ``new``, struct constructors, builtins like ``bytes.concat()`` "
"or parameters of ``abi.decode()``. A ``using for`` directive alone does not "
"contribute types. Its internal functions contribute variable types when "
"called or evaluated as function values, not merely because the directive "
"names them. Every collected type also brings in its component types, "
"recursively. The parameter lists and non-empty return lists of included "
"functions and modifiers also have tuple documents, using the same "
"representation as the lists in function types. Each parameter and return "
"variable still has its own type. The tuple describes the list, not an extra "
"variable. Including a type does not imply that a variable's runtime location "
"or value is available in the program output. Unnameable types have no "
"document. These include literal types, array slice types, types of types, "
"modifier types, magic types (such as those of ``msg`` and ``abi``), module "
"types, inaccessible dynamic types and function types other than internal and "
"external ones. Library names cannot be used as variable types, so no library "
"type document is emitted."
msgstr ""

