"``function`` with ``internal: true`` or ``external: true``, "
"``contains.parameters`` referencing a tuple and ``contains.returns`` "
"referencing a tuple when there are return values"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ethdebug.rst:160
msgid ""
"Composite types reference their components by identifier, so that the table "
"is closed: an array, a mapping or a user-defined value type refers to its "
"element, key, value or underlying type as ``{\"type\": {\"id\": \"<id>\"}}`` "
"and a struct lists its members with their ``name`` and such a reference. In "
"a function type, ``contains.parameters`` always references the tuple of "
"parameter types in declaration order. No parameters means an empty tuple. "
"One parameter means a one-element tuple, not the parameter's type on its "
"own. ``contains.returns`` follows the same convention for return types, but "
"is omitted when there are no return values. The outer tuple groups the list. "
"Classic Solidity does not support tuple-valued parameters or return "
"variables. Such a parameter or return variable would occupy one element "
"referencing another tuple rather than being flattened into the list. The "
"tuple documents themselves also reference their components by identifier. "
"Neither the parameter nor return types are inlined."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ethdebug.rst:172
msgid ""
"Structs, enums, contracts and user-defined value types carry a "
"``definition`` with the ``name`` of the definition and its ``location`` in "
"the source: the source ``id`` and the ``offset`` and ``length`` of the "
"definition. Function types in the table are types of variables, such as "
"``function (uint256) external returns (bool) f``, and are not associated "
"with a fixed function definition, so they have no ``definition``. Like in "
"source mappings, and as the schema of source ranges specifies, the offset "
"and the length count bytes of the UTF-8 encoded source, not characters."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ethdebug.rst:176
msgid ""
"A type document does not depend on where a value is stored. That is what the "
"pointer describes. The variants of a reference type in different data "
"locations therefore share one document: a ``string memory`` parameter and a "
"``string`` state variable both refer to the same entry. An import alias also "
"refers to the original type document. It does not introduce another type or "
"change the name in its ``definition``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ethdebug.rst:183
msgid "Pointer Templates"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ethdebug.rst:185
msgid ""
"The ``pointers`` table contains pointer templates (schema ``ethdebug/format/"
"pointer/template``) describing how values are laid out in storage, transient "
"storage, memory and calldata. Mutable state variables contribute their "
"storage or transient-storage layouts. Reference and mapping variables in "
"implemented functions and modifiers contribute the layouts specified by "
"their declarations. The component types of arrays, structs and mappings also "
"have templates in the enclosing value's location. Other layouts of the same "
"type are not included unless another declaration needs them. Scalar "
"parameters, return variables and locals do not add data-layout templates. "
"Constants, immutables and function or modifier declarations without a body "
"contribute type information but no pointer templates. The compiler currently "
"prefixes the canonical type identifier with the layout, for example "
"``storage-t_uint16``, ``memory-t_uint16`` and ``calldata-t_uint16``. These "
"are distinct pointer IDs referring to layouts of the same type document, "
"``t_uint16``. Pointer IDs and type IDs are not interchangeable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ethdebug.rst:196
msgid ""
"Storage and transient-storage templates expect ``slot`` as a parameter: the "
"base slot of the value they describe. Value-type templates additionally "
"expect ``offset`` as a parameter: the *little-endian offset*, counted in "
"bytes from the least significant byte, as in Solidity's storage layout. For "
"an *n*-byte value, this offset must be between 0 and ``32 - n``. Memory and "
"calldata templates instead expect ``offset`` as a parameter: the byte "
"address at which the value begins. For a dynamic array, ``bytes`` or "
"``string``, this includes the length word. For a static array or struct it "
"is the start of the first element or member. For an ABI-encoded dynamic "
"value, bind the address of its tail, not the address of the head word "
"containing the relative offset."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ethdebug.rst:204
msgid ""
"Memory templates follow the absolute pointers stored in reference-type "
"members and array elements. Calldata templates follow ABI-relative offsets, "
"and keep statically encoded structs and arrays inline. Values narrower than "
"a word are padded in memory and calldata rather than packed: numeric values "
"occupy the low-order bytes, while ``bytesN`` and external function values "
"occupy the high-order bytes. The templates describe these regions explicitly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ethdebug.rst:209
msgid ""
"Templates describe layouts, not the location or lifetime of individual "
"variables. A consumer must obtain the base address or slot, and any little-"
"endian offset, from a variable pointer or another location record before "
"instantiating a template."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ethdebug.rst:212
msgid ""
"The template of a mapping also expects ``key`` as a parameter: the key of "
"the entry it locates. The value of a mapping is not at a fixed place, and a "
"mapping nested in another type is represented by the region of its base "
"slot, from which the mapping's template locates an entry once a key is "
"bound. A key of value type is bound to the word it is hashed as, which is "
"the word Solidity holds it in on the stack: a signed integer sign-extended, "
"a ``bytesN`` in the high-order bytes followed by zeros, and any other value "
"type in the low-order bytes. The template widens a shorter value with zeros "
"on the left, which is correct for low-order-aligned keys that need zero "
"padding. A ``bytes4`` key needs padding on the right and a negative ``int8`` "
"key needs sign extension, so bind these as whole words. A ``bytes`` or "
"``string`` key is bound to its bytes, which are hashed as they are. A "
"recursive type (one nested in itself, through an array or through a mapping) "
"references its own template, which a debugger follows as far as the data "
"reaches: one element per list entry, one key per mapping level."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ethdebug.rst:220
msgid ""
"The choice of region names and pointer expressions can change between "
"compiler versions. Consumers should read them from the templates. The "
"compiler currently names regions relative to the value a template describes:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ethdebug.rst:224
msgid "the members of a struct: their names,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ethdebug.rst:225
msgid ""
"the elements of an array: ``item``, with ``index`` being the variable the "
"list iterates over,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ethdebug.rst:226
msgid "the length of a dynamic array: ``length``,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ethdebug.rst:227
msgid "a standalone value type: ``value``,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ethdebug.rst:228
msgid ""
"the parts of a storage ``bytes`` or ``string``: ``length-flag``, ``long-"
"length`` and ``data``,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ethdebug.rst:229
msgid ""
"the parts of a memory or calldata ``bytes`` or ``string``: ``length`` and "
"``data``,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ethdebug.rst:230
msgid ""
"the pointer or relative-offset word of a nested reference value: ``<member>-"
"address`` or ``item-address``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ethdebug.rst:232
msgid ""
"For non-recursive references, a template referencing the template of a "
"member's or element's type prefixes the names that one produces with the "
"member's name or ``item``, so that the ``x`` of the ``from`` member of a "
"``Line`` is ``from-x`` and that of an element of a ``Point[]`` is ``item-x``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ethdebug.rst:234
msgid ""
"// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0\n"
"pragma solidity >=0.6.0 <0.9.0;\n"
"\n"
"struct Point { uint8 x; uint8 y; }\n"
"struct Line { Point from; Point to; }\n"
"\n"
"contract C {\n"
"    uint256 total;\n"
"    Point origin;\n"
"    mapping(address => uint256) balances;\n"
"    Line line;\n"
"}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ethdebug.rst:249
msgid ""
"The following entries describe ``total``, ``origin``, entries of "
"``balances`` and ``line`` in storage. The storage layout puts ``total`` at "
"slot 0, with little-endian offset 0. A region's ``offset`` is a *big-endian "
"offset*, counted from the most significant byte of its slot. The two members "
"of ``Point`` share a slot, with ``x`` at big-endian offset 31 and ``y`` at "
"30. A debugger instantiates the ``Point`` template with ``slot`` bound to 1, "
"where the layout puts ``origin``, and the mapping's template with ``slot`` "
"bound to 2 and ``key`` to the address of the entry it wants to see. The "
"``Line`` template is instantiated with ``slot`` bound to 3. Its ``yields`` "
"maps distinguish ``from-x`` and ``from-y`` from ``to-x`` and ``to-y``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ethdebug.rst:257
msgid ""
"{\n"
"    \"storage-t_uint256\": {\n"
"        \"expect\": [\n"
"            \"slot\",\n"
"            \"offset\"\n"
"        ],\n"
"        \"for\": {\n"
"            \"location\": \"storage\",\n"
"            \"name\": \"value\",\n"
"            \"slot\": \"slot\",\n"
"            \"offset\": {\n"
"                \"~difference\": [\n"
"                    \"0x00\",\n"
"                    \"offset\"\n"
"                ]\n"
"            },\n"
"            \"length\": \"0x20\"\n"
"        }\n"
"    },\n"
"    \"storage-t_struct$_Point_$6_storage\": {\n"
"        \"expect\": [\n"
"            \"slot\"\n"
"        ],\n"
"        \"for\": {\n"
"            \"group\": [\n"
"                {\n"
"                    \"location\": \"storage\",\n"
"                    \"name\": \"x\",\n"
"                    \"slot\": \"slot\",\n"
"                    \"offset\": \"0x1f\",\n"
"                    \"length\": \"0x01\"\n"
"                },\n"
"                {\n"
"                    \"location\": \"storage\",\n"
"                    \"name\": \"y\",\n"
"                    \"slot\": \"slot\",\n"
"                    \"offset\": \"0x1e\",\n"
"                    \"length\": \"0x01\"\n"
"                }\n"
"            ]\n"
"        }\n"
"    },\n"
"    \"storage-t_mapping$_t_address_$_t_uint256_$\": {\n"
"        \"expect\": [\n"
"            \"slot\",\n"
"            \"key\"\n"
"        ],\n"
"        \"for\": {\n"
"            \"location\": \"storage\",\n"
"            \"name\": \"value\",\n"
"            \"slot\": {\n"
"                \"~keccak256\": [\n"
"                    {\n"
"                        \"~wordsized\": \"key\"\n"
"                    },\n"
"                    {\n"
"                        \"~wordsized\": \"slot\"\n"
"                    }\n"
"                ]\n"
"            }\n"
"        }\n"
"    },\n"
"    \"storage-t_struct$_Line_$13_storage\": {\n"
"        \"expect\": [\n"
"            \"slot\"\n"
"        ],\n"
"        \"for\": {\n"
"            \"group\": [\n"
"                {\n"
"                    \"template\": \"storage-t_struct$_Point_$6_storage\",\n"
"                    \"yields\": {\n"
"                        \"x\": \"from-x\",\n"
"                        \"y\": \"from-y\"\n"
"                    }\n"
"                },\n"
"                {\n"
"                    \"define\": {\n"
"                        \"slot\": {\n"
"                            \"~sum\": [\n"
"                                \"slot\",\n"
"                                \"0x01\"\n"
"                            ]\n"
"                        }\n"
"                    },\n"
"                    \"in\": {\n"
"                        \"template\": \"storage-"
"t_struct$_Point_$6_storage\",\n"
"                        \"yields\": {\n"
"                            \"x\": \"to-x\",\n"
"                            \"y\": \"to-y\"\n"
"                        }\n"
"                    }\n"
"                }\n"
"            ]\n"
"        }\n"
"    }\n"
"}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ethdebug.rst:358
msgid ""
"A region's ``offset`` is a big-endian offset, counted from the most "
"significant byte of the slot. The ``offset`` in Solidity's :ref:`storage "
"layout <storage-layout-top-level>` is a little-endian offset, counted from "
"the least significant byte. For a value of *n* bytes contained in a single "
"slot, little-endian offset *o* corresponds to big-endian offset 32 - *o* - "
"*n*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ethdebug.rst:362
msgid ""
"Solidity's offsets follow the order in which values are packed, starting at "
"the low-order end. Either direction works within a single slot, but "
"ETHDebug's `segment addressing scheme <https://github.com/ethdebug/format/"
"blob/main/schemas/pointer/scheme/segment.schema.yaml>`_ also allows a region "
"to span consecutive slots. It treats each slot as a big-endian byte "
"sequence, continuing from the least significant byte of slot ``p`` to the "
"most significant byte of slot ``p + 1``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ethdebug.rst:367
msgid "The Programs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../ethdebug.rst:369
msgid ""
"A program (schema ``ethdebug/format/program``) describes bytecode of one "
"contract: either its creation bytecode or its deployed bytecode. It names "
"the ``contract`` and the source range of its definition, states the "
"``environment`` the bytecode runs in, ``create`` or ``call``, and lists its "
"``instructions``. Every instruction carries its byte ``offset`` in the "
"bytecode, the ``operation`` with the ``mnemonic`` of the opcode and the "
"``arguments`` of a push, and, where the compiler knows it, a ``context`` "
"with the source range of the code the instruction was generated from."
msgstr ""

